{"qid":"Q577668","names":["Khanate of Bukhara"],"title":"Khanate of Bukhara","endonym":{"native":"خانات بخارا","lang":"fa"},"capsule":"The Khanate of Bukhara was a Uzbek Shaybanid successor state in Transoxiana (modern Uzbekistan and Tajikistan) founded around 1500 when Muhammad Shaybani Khan seized Samarkand from the Timurids and consolidated the region under Uzbek tribal leadership. Under Abdullah Khan II (r. 1557–1598) the khanate reached its greatest extent, capturing Badakhshan, Khorasan as far as Mashhad, and briefly Kashgar; from 1599 the Shaybanid line was replaced by the Janid (Ashtarkhanid) dynasty without a fundamental break in state structure. Persistent warfare with Safavid Persia over Khorasan and Mughal competition in Balkh drained resources throughout the 17th century; the khanate's territory contracted and in 1785 the Manghit tribal leader Shah Murad deposed the last Janid and established the Emirate of Bukhara.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Khanate of Bukhara's core territory is well documented by Timurid and Shaybanid administrative records and numerous contemporary chronicles; frontier zones in Khorasan and the Ferghana Valley fluctuated with military campaigns and are incompletely mapped.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Khanate of Bukhara","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khanate_of_Bukhara"},{"label":"Britannica: Uzbekistan","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Uzbekistan"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Khanate of Bukhara","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Muhammad Shaybani Khan","nativeName":"محمد شیبانی خان","roman":"Muḥammad Šaybānī Khān